          "question": "I get heartburn most evenings after dinner. Is that worth mentioning to a doctor?",
          "answer": "**Yes, it is worth mentioning to a doctor.** Heartburn after meals that happens most evenings is a common reason to see a clinician. Based on your description alone, the urgency is routine. That rating only means your description matched none of the emergency patterns in the red-flag guidance. It does not mean your symptoms are harmless.\n\n**Get emergency help now if you have any of these:**\n- Chest pain, pressure, or tightness, especially with breathlessness, sweating, nausea, or dizziness. Heartburn and heart pain can feel similar, and a history of heartburn does not rule out a heart cause. Do not drive yourself.\n- Pain spreading to the arm, jaw, or back.\n- Vomiting blood, or black, tarry stools (Emergency guidance on gastrointestinal bleeding).\n\n**See a doctor soon, rather than waiting for a routine visit, if you have:**\n- Trouble swallowing, or food feeling stuck.\n- Unexplained weight loss.\n- Symptoms that are new, getting worse, or no longer respond to what you have been doing.\n\nThe guidance I could retrieve does not give a full list of the warning signs for heartburn specifically, so ask your doctor which features would change the plan for you.\n\n**What to bring to the appointment:** how often it happens, what you ate, how long after meals it starts, what helps, and any other symptoms. Your doctor can decide what, if anything, to check next.\n\nNote: my first check of your wording returned an emergency result. That came from the red-flag terms I had included in the request, not from anything you described, so I re-checked using only your own description.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
